From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Jackson <noel@codewire.sh>
Date: Tue, 19 May 2026 12:42:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] xen/blkfront: recover restored transports with connected backends

On PV restore, blkfront tears down the checkpointed blkif rings and
publishes fresh ring refs/event channels to xenstore before switching the
frontend to Initialised. blkback observes that state transition, maps the
new rings, and can immediately serve rootfs I/O.

Make the ring publication explicit with a virtual-device write barrier
before the xenbus state transition. This matches the ring producer/consumer
ordering used by Xen shared rings and avoids restore races where blkback
sees the state edge before the rebuilt ring metadata is globally visible.

Also complete blkfront recovery immediately when the restored backend is
already Connected after talk_to_blkback(). Restore can recreate frontend
transport state while the backend state node is already at Connected; relying
only on a later xenbus watch edge can leave blk-mq queues stopped and wedge
the first post-restore rootfs I/O. Reading the backend state after publishing
the rebuilt frontend transport makes the resume path deterministic while the
normal watch callback remains harmless if it arrives later.

Signed-off-by: Noel Jackson <noel@codewire.sh>
---
 drivers/block/xen-blkfront.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/block/xen-blkfront.c b/drivers/block/xen-blkfront.c
index 3c5a23b1a4c1..c4257d6398a9 100644
--- a/drivers/block/xen-blkfront.c
+++ b/drivers/block/xen-blkfront.c
@@ -1878,6 +1878,15 @@ static int talk_to_blkback(struct xenbus_device *dev,
 			rinfo->shadow[j].req.u.rw.id = j + 1;
 		rinfo->shadow[BLK_RING_SIZE(info)-1].req.u.rw.id = 0x0fffffff;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * blkback maps fresh ring refs after observing Initialised, then can
+	 * immediately service rootfs I/O. Publish the rebuilt ring state before
+	 * the xenbus transition makes the transport visible to dom0. This is
+	 * especially important after PV restore, where the checkpointed rings
+	 * were torn down and replaced in-place.
+	 */
+	virt_wmb();
 	xenbus_switch_state(dev, XenbusStateInitialised);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -2059,7 +2068,9 @@ static int blkif_recover(struct blkfront_info *info)
  * driver restart.  We tear down our blkif structure and recreate it, but
  * leave the device-layer structures intact so that this is transparent to the
  * rest of the kernel.
  */
+static void blkfront_connect(struct blkfront_info *info);
+
 static int blkfront_resume(struct xenbus_device *dev)
 {
 	struct blkfront_info *info = dev_get_drvdata(&dev->dev);
@@ -2108,6 +2117,19 @@ static int blkfront_resume(struct xenbus_device *dev)
 	if (!err)
 		blk_mq_update_nr_hw_queues(&info->tag_set, info->nr_rings);
 
+	/*
+	 * Restore can rebuild the frontend transport while the backend state
+	 * is already Connected. If no new xenbus edge is delivered, waiting for
+	 * blkback_changed() leaves blk-mq queues stopped after blkif_free().
+	 * Complete the recovery here when the backend is visibly ready; a later
+	 * Connected callback is idempotent and will only refresh capacity.
+	 */
+	if (!err && xenbus_read_driver_state(dev->otherend) == XenbusStateConnected) {
+		dev_info(&dev->dev,
+			 "backend already Connected during resume; completing blkfront recovery\n");
+		blkfront_connect(info);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * We have to wait for the backend to switch to
 	 * connected state, since we want to read which
